WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.484 --> 00:00:02.460
(boat motor running)

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.050
- [Wes] He's comin' at ya.

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:06.013
Florida gators aren't afraid of much.

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:09.790
They're certainly not afraid of me.

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:10.830
But we're here to protect them

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.510
from something they should be scared of.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.600
He definitely isn't happy to see us.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.763
A sneaky little lizard from Argentina.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.180
I'm Wes Larson, a wildlife
biologist and lover

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:26.060
of the outdoors for as
long as I can remember.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:27.440
I'm traveling the country to find

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.710
the next generation of conservationists.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.340
The people on the front lines of the fight

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.090
to understand our planet and protect

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.580
the animals we share it with.

00:00:39.722 --> 00:00:42.532
(boat motor starting up)

00:00:42.532 --> 00:00:44.240
(boat motor running)

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.259
My first stop takes me all the way down

00:00:46.259 --> 00:00:48.012
to the southern wilds of Florida

00:00:48.012 --> 00:00:51.090
also known as the Everglades.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:53.500
They're huge, hot, and home to

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.860
one of the fiercest
predators on the planet:

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.183
The American alligator.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.720
These researchers from
the University of Florida

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.730
have taken me deep into the swamp

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.683
to survey the local gator population.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.500
- [Researcher] Well, we got a gator.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:08.717
- Cool.

00:01:08.717 --> 00:01:13.293
Wow, let’s see, gosh.

00:01:14.313 --> 00:01:17.490
I just gotta say, ever since I was a kid,

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:19.220
I fantasized about coming to Florida

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:21.540
and meeting alligators up close.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.630
Alligators are at the
top of the food chain.

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:24.830
Crucial to maintaining the

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:26.923
ecological balance of the Everglades.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:31.350
But despite this dominance,
alligators are under threat

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.765
from a foreign invader
that no one saw coming:

00:01:33.765 --> 00:01:36.700
The Argentine black and white tegu.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.346
Facing that threat head on are

00:01:38.346 --> 00:01:41.293
UF researchers Jenna Cole and Sarah Cooke.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:45.640
- How's it goin'?

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:46.473
- Hey, what's up?

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:47.306
- I'm Wes.

00:01:47.306 --> 00:01:48.139
- I'm Jenna, nice to meet ya.

00:01:48.139 --> 00:01:49.225
- Nice to meet you, too.

00:01:49.225 --> 00:01:52.680
(car doors closing)

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.483
- [Wes] So we are out here
lookin' for invasive reptiles.

00:01:55.483 --> 00:01:56.316
- Yes.

00:01:56.316 --> 00:01:57.510
- [Jenna] So we are gonna be looking

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:58.343
for the Argentine black and white tegu.

00:01:58.343 --> 00:02:02.210
They are just a huge problem,
preying on our native species.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:04.900
- [Wes] Tegu populations
have exploded in recent years,

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.070
so Jenna and Sarah are painstakingly

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:10.630
trapping and removing them one by one.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.750
So, how many traps are
we gonna be checking?

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.260
- [Jenna] So, we've got 160 traps out here.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:16.093
- [Wes] A long way.

00:02:16.093 --> 00:02:18.380
- [Jenna ] Yeah, it's a
pretty extensive trap line.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:20.392
- [Wes] One hundred and sixty traps spread over miles

00:02:20.392 --> 00:02:22.740
of uninhabited marshland.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.440
Where do you even begin?

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.210
Well, you start at trap number one.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:28.650
So, our first trap of the day.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.500
- Yep, this will be the first one.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.190
And it looks like we've got something.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.023
- Oh, cool.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.453
That is so crazy; he's biting at the trap.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.190
Kind of a scary little predator.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:42.360
Like a little dinosaur.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.570
- [Jenna] Yeah, essentially
they are little dinosaurs.

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:46.530
- Uh-huh, and all you had in this trap

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:47.730
to bait 'em in was a chicken egg?

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.030
- Yep, just a single chicken egg.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.261
So, if could imagine a whole
nest of alligator eggs.

00:02:52.261 --> 00:02:55.761
(melancholy guitar music)

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.640
- I mean, he just looks
like a little egg eater.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.630
But it's not only
alligators that are at risk.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:08.200
Tegus will eat just about anything.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.270
- [Sarah] The ground
nesting birds, the turtles,

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:12.970
snakes—all of those
animals are potential prey

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:15.580
for these large lizards.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:17.670
- [Wes] A single tegu
can consume an entire

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:21.460
nest of eggs in one sitting,
so we don't take any chances.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:24.190
It's straight into the bag for removal.

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:26.573
So, I'm bagging my first tegu here.

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:28.323
There we go.

00:03:28.323 --> 00:03:29.746
- [Sarah] You got it.

00:03:29.746 --> 00:03:31.836
(trap rattling)

00:03:31.836 --> 00:03:32.670
(laughter)

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:33.640
- [Jenna] Up and down like.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.180
- Up and down.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:36.320
- [Jenna] Yeah.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:37.153
- There we go.

00:03:38.744 --> 00:03:39.777
- [Sarah] Voila.

00:03:39.777 --> 00:03:40.610
- [Wes] Cool.

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:41.443
- Tah-dah.

00:03:41.443 --> 00:03:42.276
- [Wes] One down.
- [Jenna] Alright.

00:03:42.276 --> 00:03:43.710
Let's catch 160 more.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:44.560
- [Jenna] Right?

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:45.700
- We have to be very conscious of where

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:46.830
we're holding the bag 'cause they can

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:48.493
actually bite you through the bag.

00:03:48.493 --> 00:03:49.326
- [Sarah] Definitely.

00:03:49.326 --> 00:03:50.159
- [Wes] You've been bit through the bag before.

00:03:50.159 --> 00:03:50.992
- Yes.
- [Wes] Ok, cool. 

00:03:50.992 --> 00:03:53.717
- You also have to watch
if you set the bag down,

00:03:53.717 --> 00:03:55.320
they can run away in the bag.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:56.213
- Yes.

00:03:56.213 --> 00:03:57.046
- Oh, really?

00:03:57.046 --> 00:03:57.879
- So if you ever have to
set it down on the ground,

00:03:57.879 --> 00:03:58.988
we'll step on the bag
while we're doing whatever.

00:03:58.988 --> 00:03:59.821
- [Wes] You just see a bag ...

00:03:59.821 --> 00:04:00.904
- [Sarah] Oh yeah, the bag just hops away.

00:04:00.904 --> 00:04:01.737
(laughter)

00:04:01.737 --> 00:04:02.570
- [Wes] That's crazy.

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:08.960
The farther we go, the more apparent it is

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.200
just how many tegus are
crawling around out here.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.373
Man, another one down.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.600
Oh, he's excited.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.163
- [Jenna] Got another one, guys.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:23.850
- [Wes] Oh, we got another one.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.200
- [Sarah] Awesome, grumpy one.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.553
- Yeah, I can't believe how
many there are out here.

00:04:27.553 --> 00:04:28.970
There's a ton.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:31.170
- [Jenna] There is a full
breeding population of them.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:32.866
- [Wes] Saving the Everglades,
one tegu at a time.

00:04:32.866 --> 00:04:33.699
- [Sarah] One tegu at a time.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:37.550
- [Wes] This is the end of
the line for these tegus.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:39.810
Each one that Jenna and her team captures

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:42.370
is brought back to the lab and euthanized.

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:44.820
And that's tough for a
reptile-lover like Jenna.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.100
- You really have to
look at a big picture.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.690
I like tegus, and it's unfortunate

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:52.510
that there is no alternative.

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:55.570
But it's not just that we are
removing them, and that's it.

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:58.680
We are using them to learn
more about the species,

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:00.440
so that we can mitigate and prevent them

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:01.910
from causing more terrible damage

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:04.232
than what's going on already.

00:05:04.232 --> 00:05:07.482
(ominous guitar music)

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:09.828
- [Wes] Yeah, that's not a small one, huh?

00:05:09.828 --> 00:05:11.310
- No, he's not a small one.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:12.590
- A couple hundred yards from where

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:14.950
we just caught a tegu in this little canal

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:17.570
there's a pretty big alligator
just kinda hanging out.

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:20.630
So it shows that they really
are occupying the same space.

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:22.437
It's not impossible to
imagine a nest around here.

00:05:22.437 --> 00:05:23.270
- [Jenna] Oh, he's coming up.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:24.103
- [Wes] Oh, he's, yeah.

00:05:24.103 --> 00:05:25.420
- [Jenna] Heh-heh-heh.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:26.253
- Wow.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:32.320
We caught a number of
tegus today out here.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:34.010
They're from Argentina, originally.

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:35.710
So, how did they get to Florida?

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:38.930
- The general consensus
on how the tegus got here

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:40.540
is through the pet trade.

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:43.940
They were either released or
escaped into the ecosystem.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:45.610
They're very small and very cute

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:48.220
when they are first
sold and first hatched.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.500
And as the grow people tend to realize

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:54.253
that they may be in for a lot
more than they bargained for.

00:05:55.371 --> 00:05:57.720
- And then, in the middle of the swamp,

00:05:57.720 --> 00:05:59.970
we come across one very special tegu.

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:04.880
So we've got a tegu here
that has a transmitter on it.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:07.140
So, I believe, that means that we actually

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.230
get to pull him out after we bag him,

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:11.020
and I actually get to get my hands on him.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:11.853
That's right, right?

00:06:11.853 --> 00:06:13.540
- Yep, we're gonna go
ahead and get him out.

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:14.750
- [Wes] In this vast wilderness,

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:16.300
the research team has transmitters

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.741
on only six tegus, so it's
a real stroke of luck

00:06:18.741 --> 00:06:20.203
that we found this one.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:25.740
- [Cameraman] What do you think, Wes?

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:27.040
- Pretty cool.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:28.620
- Yeah, I was gonna say, when they squirm,

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:29.600
you don't grab the tail.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:30.433
- Yeah.

00:06:30.433 --> 00:06:31.720
And that's just 'cause it can detach?

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:32.820
- Yeah, he'll drop it.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:35.955
- I can definitely tell that
there's some real strength.

00:06:35.955 --> 00:06:39.990
We've obviously got a tegu out of the trap

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:41.620
that we're handling, so why is it

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:43.470
that we're able to pull this one out?

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:45.034
- We are actually following him around

00:06:45.034 --> 00:06:48.290
and learning more about where he lives

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:49.992
and what he's doing out here, so that we

00:06:49.992 --> 00:06:52.490
can learn more ways to effectively

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:54.650
trap and remove them from
this Everglades system.

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:56.140
- He's like a little spy for you guys?

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:59.660
- Yeah, so he's like a double agent.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:01.150
- [Wes] It's hard to
believe that something

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:02.925
as small as releasing some pet lizards

00:07:02.925 --> 00:07:05.863
can threaten the health
of an entire ecosystem.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.360
My time here has proven
to me just how delicate

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.110
that balance can be.

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:13.030
The scientists we met from UF are fighting

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:15.560
a daily battle to right
some of our wrongs.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:18.320
It's hot, humid and frustrating work,

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:21.095
but these biologists care
deeply about native species

00:07:21.095 --> 00:07:23.090
and tagging along helped me understand

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:24.840
just how important their battle is.

00:07:26.076 --> 00:07:28.826
(frogs croaking)

